{"date":"2026-09-11","heading":"September 11, 2026","editions":[{"edition":"morning","byline":"Dawn patrol","text":"The wire opened its day with a Tufted Titmouse at 07:17, just six minutes after sunrise. By 08:00, the feeder had already recorded 87 visits, a figure that stands out sharply against the recent daily average of 19.2 for the same time. The station’s first confident visual record of the American Goldfinch arrived during this steady climb.\n\nThe auditory landscape was equally active, with Carolina Wrens dominating the counts with 48 detections. American Crows contributed 35 of their own. This level of vocal activity, totaling 101 detections by 10:30, exceeded the recent average of 60.8.\n\nThe first three hours saw a steady rotation of Tufted Titmice and Carolina Chickadees, with the latter making 22 confident visits. A White-breasted Nuthatch appeared around 08:09.","time":"10:30"},{"edition":"afternoon","byline":"Field notebook","text":"American Crows and Eastern Towhees dominate the midday auditory counts, while the feeder sees fewer Tufted Titmice and Carolina Chickadees. A Ruby-throated Hummingbird is the last visual arrival at 12:56.\n\nBy 15:30, the station records 95 total visits and 130 auditory detections, exceeding recent daily averages for this time. The Carolina Wren leads with 58 detections. The weather holds steady at 30.1 C with no precipitation.\n\nThe American Goldfinch was first seen earlier today. The station has 13 distinct species, exceeding the recent average of 8.0.","time":"15:30"},{"edition":"evening","byline":"Closing entry","text":"The day concludes with 107 feeder visits, a figure more than triple the recent daily average of 33. American Crows and Carolina Wrens provided the final auditory presence, with the crow’s last visual record at 19:44 marking the end of the day's observations.","time":"20:30"}],"sunrise":"07:11","sunset":"19:44","weather":{"high_c":30.2,"wind_kph":5.258333333333334,"precip_mm":4.0},"counts":{"species"